---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ChapterNav from '../components/ChapterNav.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="National Health Assurance: Risk">
  <main>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>From protections to failure modes</h2>
    <p class="desc">The Executive Hardening chapter shows the protections built
      to keep the system running under attack. It does not say, in one place,
      what actually breaks and how badly. This chapter does. It is a Failure
      Modes and Effects Analysis: it treats every controlled target as a
      promise that can be broken, states what happens when it is, and ranks
      each break by how likely it is and how much harm it causes.</p>
    <div class="fmea-premise">
      <span>The definition of a failure used here</span>
      <p><strong>A failure mode is a phase-based target that is not met.</strong>
        Every key performance parameter and technical performance parameter
        carries a target at each rollout phase where it can be measured. Each
        of those phase targets is one way the system can fall short. Cost
        parameters carry no phase target, so their failure is a different
        thing: the controlled quantity is never calibrated, or it is
        calibrated outside its allowed range.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="fmea-scope" aria-label="What the analysis covers">
      <article>
        <span class="fmea-kicker">System outcomes</span>
        <strong id="fmea-n-kpp">KPP</strong>
        <b>failure modes across all measurable phases</b>
        <small>Coverage, access, safety, equity, fiscal, transition, and workforce targets, one failure per phase.</small>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="fmea-kicker">Operating performance</span>
        <strong id="fmea-n-tpp">TPP</strong>
        <b>failure modes across all measurable phases</b>
        <small>Eligibility, payment, medicines, facilities, data, cyber, rights, and innovation targets, one failure per phase.</small>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="fmea-kicker">Cost and model control</span>
        <strong id="fmea-n-cp">CP</strong>
        <b>calibration-tolerance failure modes</b>
        <small>One per cost parameter: the value is never calibrated or lands outside its controlled range.</small>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="note"><b>Why every phase and not just maturity.</b> A target that
      is safe to miss in a small Year 6 pilot can be catastrophic to miss once
      the same service is national. Assessing the target at every phase is what
      lets the analysis separate a recoverable pilot shortfall from a
      system-level harm, and it is why the same parameter can appear in more
      than one risk band.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>How each failure is scored</h2>
    <p class="desc">Every failure mode is placed on a five-by-five risk chart.
      One axis is probability, how likely the target is to be missed. The other
      is consequence, how much harm the miss causes. Both are derived from
      signals already in the controlled catalog, not asserted. The cell where
      the two axes meet sets the color and the criticality tier.</p>
    <div class="fmea-scales">
      <div class="fmea-scale">
        <h3>Probability (occurrence)</h3>
        <ul id="fmea-prob-scale"></ul>
        <p class="fmea-scale-note">Raised by target stringency, by the size of
          the required phase-to-phase improvement, by the parameter not yet
          being calibrated, and by the unproven foundation phases.</p>
        <p class="fmea-scale-note" id="fmea-prob-floor"></p>
      </div>
      <div class="fmea-scale">
        <h3>Consequence (severity)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>5</b> Catastrophic: irreversible or mass harm, or system solvency.</li>
          <li><b>4</b> Severe: serious harm to many, recoverable with effort.</li>
          <li><b>3</b> Moderate: localized or recoverable harm.</li>
          <li><b>2</b> Minor: degraded performance, readily corrected.</li>
          <li><b>1</b> Negligible.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="fmea-scale-note">Set by the kind of harm the miss produces,
          reduced for a technical enabler versus a system outcome, scaled by
          how many people the phase reaches, and raised by one where the target
          is a phase gate that halts a rollout wave.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="note"><b>When the catalog cannot answer, a parameter is missing.</b>
      Cost parameters have no controlled likelihood attribute, so their
      probability had to be borrowed from the confidence grade of the modeled
      quantity they calibrate. Seven outcome targets were deliberately left as
      a number to be set later, so their probability cannot be assessed against
      a real value at all. Both cases are reported below as parameter gaps
      rather than filled with an invented number. A secondary risk priority
      number multiplies consequence, probability, and how hard the failure is
      to detect, and is shown on each record.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>The five-by-five risk chart</h2>
    <p class="desc">This chart holds the phase-target failures, the ones that
      break when a key or technical performance target is missed. Each cell
      counts the failures at that probability and consequence. Color follows the
      standard risk grid: green is low, yellow moderate, orange high, red
      extreme. Cost-parameter calibration risk is charted separately below,
      because its probability is borrowed rather than native. Select any cell to
      filter the explorer at the foot of the page.</p>
    <div id="fmea-matrix" class="fmea-matrix-wrap" aria-label="Five by five probability and consequence risk chart"></div>
    <div id="fmea-headlines" class="fmea-headlines" aria-label="The most probable, most consequential, and both"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Effects grouped by criticality</h2>
    <p class="desc">The same failures, grouped by their risk tier and then by
      the kind of harm the effect produces. The critical tier is where a likely
      miss meets a catastrophic effect. Reading down the tiers shows where the
      program should spend its detection, staffing, and reserve attention
      first.</p>
    <div id="fmea-tiers" class="fmea-tiers" aria-label="Failure modes grouped by criticality tier"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Cost-parameter calibration risk</h2>
    <p class="desc">Cost parameters carry no phase target, so their failure is a
      calibration-tolerance breach: the value is never calibrated or lands
      outside its controlled range. They sit on their own chart because their
      probability is not native to the controlled catalog. It is borrowed from
      the confidence grade of the modeled quantity each family calibrates, so a
      low-confidence estimate reads as more likely to miss. Consequence is still
      scored from the ledger's domain and model role.</p>
    <div id="fmea-cp" class="fmea-cp" aria-label="Cost-parameter calibration risk chart and ledger families"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Where the analysis needs a new parameter</h2>
    <p class="desc">A failure mode you cannot score is itself a finding: the
      controlled catalog is missing the information needed to manage that risk.
      Two gaps appear. Cost parameters have no native likelihood control, and a
      small set of outcome targets were deferred as numbers to be set later.
      Each is listed with the parameter that would close it.</p>
    <div id="fmea-gaps" class="fmea-gaps" aria-label="Parameter gaps revealed by the analysis"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Explore every failure mode</h2>
    <p class="desc">Search the parameter or its text, narrow by type, concept,
      phase, or risk band, and select any row to see its failure mode, its
      effect, the basis for each score, and its risk priority number.</p>
    <div class="fmea-controls" role="group" aria-label="Failure mode filters">
      <label class="fmea-control fmea-search">
        <span>Search</span>
        <input id="fmea-search" type="search" placeholder="ID, parameter, effect, owner…">
      </label>
      <label class="fmea-control">
        <span>Parameter type</span>
        <select id="fmea-type">
          <option value="all">All types</option>
          <option value="KPP">KPP: key performance</option>
          <option value="TPP">TPP: technical performance</option>
          <option value="CP">CP: cost parameter</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <label class="fmea-control">
        <span>Concept</span>
        <select id="fmea-concept"><option value="all">All concepts</option></select>
      </label>
      <label class="fmea-control">
        <span>Rollout phase</span>
        <select id="fmea-phase">
          <option value="all">All phases</option>
          <option value="P0">P0 (Year 1)</option>
          <option value="P1">P1 (Year 2)</option>
          <option value="P2">P2 (Year 3)</option>
          <option value="P3">P3 (Year 4)</option>
          <option value="P4">P4 (Year 6)</option>
          <option value="P5">P5 (Year 7)</option>
          <option value="P6">P6 (Year 8)</option>
          <option value="P7">P7 (Year 10)</option>
          <option value="P8">P8 (Year 12)</option>
          <option value="calibration">Calibration (CP)</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <label class="fmea-control">
        <span>Risk band</span>
        <select id="fmea-band">
          <option value="all">All bands</option>
          <option value="extreme">Extreme (critical)</option>
          <option value="high">High (serious)</option>
          <option value="moderate">Moderate</option>
          <option value="low">Low (minor)</option>
        </select>
      </label>
    </div>
    <div class="fmea-filter-foot">
      <p id="fmea-results" aria-live="polite"></p>
      <button id="fmea-reset" class="btn" type="button">Reset filters</button>
    </div>

    <div id="fmea-selected" class="fmea-selected" aria-live="polite" aria-label="Selected failure mode"></div>

    <div class="tbl-scroll fmea-table-wrap">
      <table class="data fmea-table" id="fmea-table">
        <caption class="sr-only">Every derived failure mode with probability, consequence, and risk</caption>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <p class="rollout-source">Failure modes are derived from the controlled key
    performance, technical performance, and cost parameter catalogs and their
    phase targets. Probability and consequence are scored from catalog signals;
    each record shows its own basis.
    <a href="https://github.com/mikehalvorson/US-National-Health-Assurance-System" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sources and methodology</a>.</p>

    <ChapterNav />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import '../scripts/fmea-client.ts';
</script>
